    "body": "The status quo is that it is possible to perform deanonymization by examining the metadata of uploaded files (I tried the demo interface and saw no hints about it). A whistleblower may be unaware of possible embedded GPS coordinates in JPG files, or author names in Word documents, etc.\r\n\r\nGlobaleaks would give _a false sense of anonymity_, while the journalists who receive the documents will be able to connect the dots and find the identity of the submitter. Even worse, the police could raid the offices of a newspaper and confiscate all the computers, to do forensic analysis. The authorities would find the source of the leak even if the journalists themselves had no intention to help them.\r\n\r\nThere is a related issue here: #1323, but meta-data is potentially more dangerous.\r\n\r\nI think that in an ideal case we could:\r\n\r\n- tell people about the dangers of metadata _before_ they submit anything\r\n- use client-side libraries to remove metadata before the data gets to the server. This should be done, at least for the most common formats like PDF, JPG, Word, Excel, Powerpoint\r\n- perhaps even display a summary of what metadata was found and removed before the submission.\r\n- metadata could be removed on the server too (though it implies submitters have to trust the server admins - which is too much to ask, in my opinion)",
